{"content":[{"c":["TARTAN*"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Title of the highest-ranking Assyrian official, second in command only to the king. The tartan was the commander in chief of the Assyrian army. The position is mentioned in two OT passages: (1) Sargon II, king of Assyria (722–705 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},"), ordered his commander to subdue and capture the Philistine city of Ashdod (",{"href":"?bref=Isa.20.1","r":"Isa.20.1","rawR":"Isa.20.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.20.1","value":"Isa.20.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"Is 20:1"},"). (2) In ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.8.17","r":"2Kgs.8.17","rawR":"2Kgs.8.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.8.17","value":"2Kgs.8.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kings 8:17"}," the tartan was one of three officials whom King Sennacherib of Assyria (705–681 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},") posted over the Assyrian army sent from Lachish to Jerusalem to confront King Hezekiah of Judah (715–687 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},"). "],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Tartan","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Is 20:1","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Isa.20.1","rawQueryValue":"Isa.20.1","sourceRefExact":"Isa.20.1"},{"anchorText":"2 Kings 8:17","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Kgs.8.17","rawQueryValue":"2Kgs.8.17","sourceRefExact":"2Kgs.8.17"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"b1719527543d6a8bae31c83e5b09c45668c904a775b0e6aec8879228e4cf7466","sourceItemName":"Tartan","sourceItemOrder":61,"sourceMember":"Articles/T.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Tartan","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTitleExact":"Tartan*","title":"Tartan*","version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}